<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>eu.wohlben.qits</groupId>
        <artifactId>qits-agents</artifactId>
        <version>2026.909.122712</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>qits-coding-agents</artifactId>
    <name>qits-agents :: agents</name>

    <!-- The coding-agent harnesses (Claude Code, Kimi) and everything that reads their state:
         launch-spec rendering, the ACP transport, session lineage, transcript reads, plugin install,
         prompt refinement and auth status.

         The union of two copies. What each side had alone came with it: AgentDesk and its tickets
         steering from the projects copy, AgentPluginService/InstalledPluginDto/PromptRefinementService
         from the workspace copy, and AgentMcpScope's ACTIONS value from the workspace copy beside
         PROJECT and REPOSITORY from both.

         What is NOT here is what made the two copies different rather than merely diverged: the
         scope-to-server mapping. The projects daemon attaches exactly one MCP server (`repository`),
         the workspace daemon attaches three (`repository`, `observability`, `actions`), each with
         its own narrowing and its own pre-approval list, and those are policy about which product
         the session is inside. AgentMcpServers is that seam; AgentLaunchService renders whatever it
         is handed and has stopped knowing.

         Depends on qits-commands because launching an agent *is* launching a command: the
         AgentLaunchService -> CommandService direction, never the other way round. Framework-free
         for the same native-image reason as its sibling, and with no resources directory at all —
         which is why a steering prompt is a Java text block a test asserts byte for byte.

         The workspace copy also declared `workspace-daemon-files`. Nothing in the moved code
         imports it (checked class by class), so it did not come along: a dependency that only the
         pom knows about would have made this library need one daemon's jar to build. -->

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-commands</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- io.vertx.core.json only: the ACP wire, the harness transcript JSONL and the plugin
                 manifests are all JSON. Same reasoning as qits-commands. -->
            <groupId>io.vertx</groupId>
            <artifactId>vertx-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
